{
  "summary": "Reset the request ID and JSON-RPC version fields at the start of JSON-RPC request parsing. This prevents malformed non-object elements in a batch from inheriting the ID and version of preceding elements in error responses.",
  "problem": "When processing a JSON-RPC batch request, the server reuses a single request structure across elements. If a batch element is not a JSON object, parsing throws before clearing the ID and version, causing the error response to reuse the previous call's ID or omit the error entirely if the previous call was a notification.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: \"For `[{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"getblockcount\",\"id\":\"X\"}, 2]` the reply has two objects with `\"id\":\"X\"`, one with a result and one with an error. If the first element is a notification, the error is dropped.\"",
    "PR description: \"The new case in `interface_rpc.py` fails on master and passes with this change.\"",
    "Commit message: \"A client that matches replies by id can take that error for the result of the previous call.\""
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Very small change (+19/-0) touching RPC request parsing and adding test coverage in interface_rpc.py.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "Reset request ID and JSON-RPC version at the beginning of request parsing rather than after validating object types. In a JSON-RPC batch, a non-object element currently throws before clearing those fields, leading to error responses that carry the preceding element's ID or dropping errors if the preceding element was a notification. The author notes that batch clients matching by ID can confuse the error with the prior call's result, and includes a test in interface_rpc.py reproducing the failure.",
